                        %mi H
                        %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{I}_X^{H k}
              and the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+chain+complex"} singular chain complex
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              for ordinary homology with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coefficients"} coefficients
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              This is a classical basic (maybe
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/folklore"} folklore
            %p
              First notice that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-colimit"} (∞,1)-colimit
              of functors out of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-groupoids"} ∞-groupoids
              and constant on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (H k)Mod
              is by definition the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-tensoring"} (∞,1)-tensoring
              operation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (H k)Mod
              over
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9EGrpd"} ∞Grpd
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presentation
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (H k)Mod
              by a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
              the by the dicussion at
              %em
                %a{:href => "limit+in+a+quasi-category#ModelsForTensoring"} (∞,1)-colomit – Tensoring and cotensoring – Models
              this
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-tensoring"} (∞,1)-tensoring
              is given by the left
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
              of the
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensoring"} tensoring
              in that simplicial model category.
            %p
              To obtain this, use prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DoldKan"}
              and then the discussion at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
              in the section
              %em
                %a{:href => "model%20structure%20on%20chain%20complexes#ProjectiveModelStructureOnUnboundedChainComplexes"} Projective model structure on unbounded chain complexes
              which says that there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
              structure on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+simplicial+objects"} category of simplicial objects
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+unbounded+chain+complexes"} category of unbounded chain complexes
              which models
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi L
                        %mi qi
                      %msub
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %mi Mod
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_{qi} Ch_\bullet(k Mod)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
              under the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+chain+complex"} total chain complex
              functor.
            %p
              In summary it follows that with any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo •
                    %mi in
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi whe
                    %mi sSet
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Pi(X))_\bullet in L_{whe} sSet
              representing
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X)
              (under the
              = succeed "-theorem)" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+hypothesis"} homotopy hypothesis
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝕀
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                        %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi 𝕀
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\underset{\to}{\lim} (\mathbb{I}_X^{H k})
                    \\simeq
                    \\int_{[n] \in \Delta}  (\Pi(X))_n \cdot \mathbb{I}
                    \\,,
            %p
              where on the right we have the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coend"} coend
              over the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensoring"} tensoring
              (of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+objects"} simplicial objects
              in the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+unbounded+chain+complexes"} category of unbounded chain complexes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+unit"} tensor unit
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            %p
              The result on the right is manifestly, by the very definition of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+homology"} singular homology
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
              the
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex+of+singular+simplices"} chain complex of singular simplices
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∫
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                        %mi n
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi Δ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi n
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi 𝕀
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi Π
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\int_{[n] \in \Delta}  (\Pi(X))_n \cdot \mathbb{I}
                    \\simeq
                    \\Pi(X) \otimes k
                    \\simeq
                    C_\bullet(X,k)
                    \\,.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mi whe
                    %mi Top
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in L_{whe} Top
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality+space"} Poincaré duality space
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi 𝕀
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mi H
                        %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi H
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Mod
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma(\mathbb{I}_X^{H A}) \in (H A) Mod
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+object"} dualizable object
              which is almost self-dual except for a degree
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+cohomology"} twist
              of (itself) degree 0:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %mi Γ
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %msubsup
                            %mi 𝕀
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mi H
                              %mi A
                          %mo )
                        %mo )
                      %mo ∨
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi Σ
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %mi Γ
                      %mrow
                        %mo (
                        %msubsup
                          %mi 𝕀
                          %mi X
                          %mrow
                            %mi H
                            %mi A
                        %mo )
                      %mo )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\left(
                    \\Gamma\left(\mathbb{I}_X^{H A}\right)
                    \\right)^\vee
                    \\simeq
                    \\Sigma^{-n}
                    \\left(
                    \\Gamma\left(\mathbb{I}_X^{H A}\right)
                    \\right)
                    \\,.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Under the identifications of prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DoldKan"}
              and prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SectionsOfTrivialKABundleIsAHomology"}
              this is the theorem discussed at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poincar%C3%A9+duality"} Poincaré duality
              in the section
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "Poincar%C3%A9+duality#RefinementInHomotopyTheory"} Poincaré duality – Refinement to homotopy theory
          %p
            See also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Thom+theorem"} Dold-Thom theorem
          %h3#ordinary_homology_spectra_split Ordinary homology spectra split
          %p
            see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+homology+spectra+split"} ordinary homology spectra split
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+homology"}> singular homology
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+homology"} Čech homology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cellular+homology"} cellular homology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Thom+theorem"} Dold-Thom theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+topology"} string topology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontrjagin+ring"} Pontrjagin ring
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+homology+theory"} stable homotopy homology theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bordism+homology+theory"} bordism homology theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pochhammer+loop"} Pochhammer loop
          %h2#references References
          %p A classical account:
          %ul
            %li#Switzer75
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Switzer"}> Robert Switzer
              , chapter 10 of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic Topology - Homotopy and Homology
          %p
            In terms of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/current+%28distribution+theory%29"} currents
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+de+Rham"}> Georges de Rham
              , Chapter IV of:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Differentiable Manifolds – Forms, Currents, Harmonic Forms
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 266
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-642-61752-2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-642-61752-2
          %p Textbook accounts:
          %ul
            %li#Bredon93
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Glen+Bredon"}> Glen Bredon
                , Chapter IV of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Topology and Geometry
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 139
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-6848-0"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6848-0
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://virtualmath1.stanford.edu/~ralph/math215b/Bredon.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Hatcher02
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Allen+Hatcher"}> Allen Hatcher
                , §2 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic Topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cambridge.org/gb/academic/subjects/mathematics/geometry-and-topology/algebraic-topology-1?format=PB&isbn=9780521795401"} ISBN:9780521795401
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://pi.math.cornell.edu/~hatcher/AT/ATpage.html"} webpage
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Anatoly+Fomenko"}> Anatoly Fomenko
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Fuchs"} Dmitry Fuchs
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homotopical Topology
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 273
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-23488-5"} doi:10.1007/978-3-319-23488-5
                = succeed "]" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.cimat.mx/~gil/docencia/2020/topologia_diferencial/[Fomenko,Fuchs]Homotopical_Topology(2016).pdf"} pdf
          %p Comprehensive monograph:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jean+Gallier"}> Jean Gallier
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jocelyn+Quaintance"} Jocelyn Quaintance
              = succeed "," do
                %em Homology, Cohomology, and Sheaf Cohomology for Algebraic Topology, Algebraic Geometry, and Differential Geometry
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/12495"} doi:10.1142/12495
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://www.cis.upenn.edu/~jean/gbooks/sheaf-coho.html"} webpage
          %p
            With an eye towards application in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mathematical+physics"} mathematical physics
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mikio+Nakahara"}> Mikio Nakahara
              , Chapter 3 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Geometry%2C+Topology+and+Physics"} Geometry, Topology and Physics
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1201/9781315275826"} doi:10.1201/9781315275826
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://alpha.sinp.msu.ru/~panov/LibBooks/GRAV/(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)Mikio_Nakahara-Geometry,_Topology_and_Physics,_Second_Edition_(Graduate_Student_Series_in_Physics)-Institute_of_Physics_Publishing(2003).pdf"} pdf
          %p
            The
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann-Hilbert+correspondence"} Riemann-Hilbert correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Rham+theorem"} de Rham theorem
            for
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H A
          %ul
            %li#BlockSmith09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jonathan+Block"}> Jonathan Block
              , Aaron Smith,
              %em A Riemann–Hilbert correspondence for infinity local systems
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0908.2843"}> arXiv:0908.2843
              )
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenzo"} Kenzo
            software for homology computations in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+algebraic+topology"} constructive algebraic topology
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Julio+Rubio"}> Julio Rubio
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Sergeraert"} Francis Sergeraert
              %em KENZO – a Symbolic Software for Effective Homology Computation
              (1999) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://www-fourier.ujf-grenoble.fr/~sergerar/Kenzo/Kenzo-doc.pdf"}> pdf
              \&amp;rbrack;
